\b 26:1\u8211?11\b0 
When you come into the land, take the first of all the fruit in a basket, go to the chosen place, and speak the confession: \u8216?a Syrian ready to perish was my father\u8217? (Egypt, exodus, land). \u8216?Rejoice in every good thing.\u8217?
firstfruits \u183? arami oved avi \u183? rejoice \u183? received \u8594? given back
Why does a story belong with the bringing of the fruit?


\b 26:12\u8211?19\b0 
The third-year tithe for Levite, stranger, fatherless and widow (\u8216?I have hearkened\u8217?); and the mutual declaration: you have said the LORD is your God, the LORD has said you are His own people (am segulah).
I have hearkened \u183? you / the LORD have said \u183? am segulah
Who speaks first here \u8212? you, or God?


\b 27:11\u8211?26\b0 
Gerizim and Ebal; twelve curses, mostly over hidden sins (the graven image in secret, misleading the blind), each answered with \u8216?Amen\u8217?.
hidden sins \u183? \u8216?Amen\u8217? \u183? wholeness (inside = outside)
Why curses precisely over what no one sees?


\b 28 (hinge 28:47)\b0 
Blessing on obedience; the long, swelling curse on disobedience; and the key: \u8216?because thou servedst not the LORD thy God with joyfulness and with gladness of heart, for the abundance of all things\u8217? (28:47).
blessing / curse \u183? joyless service \u183? \u8216?for the abundance\u8217?
How can abundance become the root of the curse?


\b 29:1\u8211?9 (hinge 29:4)\b0 
You have seen it all \u8212? the signs, the wonders, the forty years \u8212? \u8216?yet the LORD hath not given you an heart to perceive, and eyes to see, and ears to hear, unto this day\u8217? (29:4).
\u8216?no heart to perceive\u8217? \u183? gift \u183? \u8216?unto this day\u8217?
If even the heart is given, what remains of \u8216?achieving\u8217??


\b Isa. 60:1 / Matt. 4:16\b0 
\u8216?Arise, shine; for thy light is come\u8217? \u8212? the light rises upon you and the nations come to it. And: \u8216?the people which sat in darkness saw great light\u8217? (citing Isa. 9).
received light reflected back \u183? the eyes open
How does \u8216?arise, shine\u8217? relate to \u8216?no heart\u8217? in 29:4?




\par\b\fs28 A2. Connecting hypotheses\b0\fs24\par


\par \b \u8594?\b0  \i Formulate two readings the text could carry, and choose with the test: which explains the most data with the least force?\i0 \par


\par \b Hypothesis 1 (one axis):\b0  the center of gravity is the Will axis alone \u8212? the parashah is covenant response: bring, confess, remember, obey, rejoice, answer \u8216?Amen\u8217?. The gift side then stays background.\par


\par \b Hypothesis 2 (relation):\b0  the center of gravity is the relation between Source and Will \u8212? all is gift (down to the heart, 29:4), and precisely therefore the response is thankfulness, not achievement. The firstfruits embody receiving-and-giving-back in one act.\par


\par \b Which explains the most data with the least force?\b0  Hypothesis 2. Hypothesis 1 sees the response, but makes am segulah (26:18, God pronounces the status) and 29:4 (the heart is given) inexplicable. The relation reading binds the firstfruits, 28:47 and 29:4 into one figure.\par


\par \b What would refute this reading?\b0  If the firstfruits (front), 28:47 (mid-curse) and 29:4 (end) turned out not to be one coherent figure but loose rules, the center of gravity would lie elsewhere \u8212? for instance at the integrity of 27\u8211?28 (Coherence).\par


\par\b\fs28 A3. Four axes\b0\fs24\par


\par \b \u8594?\b0  \i Fill in the text anchors and a short diagnosis per axis. Leave the last column for what your reading does not smooth over \u8212? the open anomaly.\i0 \par










